<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panjikent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Penjikent, Pendzhikent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panjikent is a town in north-western Tajikistan on the Zarafshan river, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. Beside it lie the ruins of ancient Panjikent, a Sogdian town of the 5th to 8th centuries whose excavated houses and temples have yielded extensive wall paintings of epics, gods and banquets. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a> was born nearby, and his mausoleum stands in the district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panjikent.jpg" alt="Panjikent" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panjakent,RudakiMuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at Bukhara, <b>Panjikent</b> and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heart was the fertile valley of the Zeravshan, which the Greeks called the Polytimetus, with the oases of Samarkand, Bukhara and <b>Panjikent</b>; to the south lay the valley of the Kashka Darya, with Kesh and Nakhshab.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Panjikent</b>, on the Zarafshan River east of Samarkand, was founded in the 5th century and grew into a walled town of merchants and landowners.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-panjikent">Principality of Panjikent</a></li></ul></section>
